  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="NL">Hands Off eSwatini</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="NL">To reduce violence against sex workers, Aidsfonds works together with sex worker-led group VooV. They will build a strong national movement and will provide prevention, care and support services. Human rights violations against sex workers will be documented and used for advocacy. The organisations will advocate for law reform and policy and service provision changes.   Relationships with key stakeholders, such as service providers, community and religious leaders and police officers will be sought or intensified to reduce stigma and discrimination. 

Main objectives and outcomes:
Hands Off II partners work to reduce violence against sex workers at community, national and regional level. Outcomes the organisations work on mainly include: an empowered and resilient sex worker movement demands its rights; increased access to and use of inclusive services for sex workers; and a more enabling and supportive environment for sex work.

Description of target groups: 
The programme&#x2019;s primary target group is female, male and transgender sex workers, meaning those who receive money or goods in exchange for sexual services, either regularly or occasionally. The organisations will also work with lawyers, government officials, policymakers and service providers.

Additional information:
 Sex work in Eswatini exists in a similarly ambiguous legal landscape, where the act itself is not directly criminalized, but related activities such as brothel-keeping and solicitation are illegal, contributing to a precarious and often dangerous environment for sex workers. This population faces significant challenges, including high levels of stigma, discrimination, and violence, both from clients and law enforcement. Health issues, particularly the high prevalence of HIV, are a major concern, compounded by limited access to healthcare and social services. The marginalization of sex workers in Eswatini exacerbates their vulnerability, making it difficult for them to seek protection and support.</narrative>
  </description>
